course: dictionary
lesson: 456
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The word arm can mean different things. Context, the words around it, tells you which meaning is right. «She rested her arm on the arm of the sofa.» The first arm is part of her body. The second arm is the part of the sofa where you rest your arm.
explain: «The arm of the crane swung slowly over the building site.» A crane has no shoulders or hands, so arm must mean a long part that sticks out. Always ask: what would fit here? Then try your meaning in the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: Some uses of arm are phrases. «The two friends walked arm in arm down the lane.» Arm in arm means linked together, not that their arms were detached from anything. When a phrase puzzles you, read the whole sentence before you decide.
fact: «Arm» means one of the two long body parts from your shoulder to your hand.
fact: «Arm» can also mean the part of a chair or sofa where you rest your arm, or the sleeve of a coat or shirt.
fact: «Arm» can mean a long part that sticks out, such as the arm of a crane or an arm of the sea.
fact: «Arms» can mean weapons, and «arm» as a verb means to give weapons.
fact: Phrases such as «arm in arm» (linked together) and «an arm and a leg» (very expensive) do not mean what each word says alone.
exercise: «She rested her arm on the arm of the chair.» What does the second arm mean? | *The part of a chair where you rest your arm | A sleeve of a coat | A long body part from shoulder to hand | A weapon
exercise: «Keep your arms inside the car window.» What does arms mean here? | Weapons | The sleeves of a coat | *The long body parts from shoulder to hand | The arm of a crane
exercise: «The arm of the crane swung slowly over the site.» What does arm mean here? | A sleeve | A body part | To give weapons | *A long part that sticks out
test: «The soldiers were ordered to arm themselves before the battle.» What does arm mean in this sentence? | *To get weapons | To bend the elbow | To hold something tightly | To wave a hand
test: «Ravi and Sam walked arm in arm down the lane.» What does arm in arm mean? | Arguing loudly | Carrying each other's bags | Running in a race | *Linked together, with arms through each other's
exam: «The physio gave exercises to bend and strengthen Mia's arm.» What does arm mean here? | A sleeve | The arm of a chair | *One of the two long body parts from your shoulder to your hand | Weapons
